<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Plan 53 §7 B3 (was X11). SPLITTING A COMPED COLUMN KEEPS THE COMP ON BOTH
  HALVES, AND UNSPLIT PUTS THE ORIGINAL MAP BACK.

  ssplitclipaction.cpp's direct branch built the tail STakeStack without a map
  at all, and left the head's segments beyond the split in place. So a split
  threw the comp away on the tail and left the head describing frames it no
  longer has.

  THE ACTIVE TAKE IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE MAPPED TAKE. This is what stops the
  case being vacuous: an empty map MEANS "ask activeTake_", so if the active
  take were the one the map selects, a dropped map would render EXACTLY the
  same audio and every assertion below would pass against the broken code.
  Take 0 is active throughout; the map selects take 1 over most of the column.

  THE NUMBERS ARE MEASURED, NOT INVENTED. They come from comp_map_audible.qxa,
  which established them for this fixture: test_gapsaw.wav is a 0.1 → 0.8 ramp
  with exact-zero regions, take 0 is unslipped and take 1 is slipped one
  second, and their per-second RMS over the four windows is

      window        take 0 (active)   take 1 (mapped)
      [0,1) s       0.0849            0.2115
      [3,4) s       0.4125            0.0  (the file's own silence)

  so both windows separate the two takes completely, and windows 2 and 3 are
  the ones where the ramp makes them agree — never asserted here.

  WINDOW 4 ALSO GATES THE TAIL'S SHIFT ARITHMETIC, which is the part most
  likely to be got wrong. The map is take 1 from 0, take 0 from 48000 and take
  1 again from 144000; the split is at 96000, so the tail's segments must come
  out as

      { 0 → take 0 },  { 48000 → take 1 }        (144000 − 96000 = 48000)

  the opening segment being whatever sounded AT the split. THREE distinct
  failures are then audible in window 4, which asserts the file's own silence:

    • the tail's map DROPPED → falls back to active take 0 → 0.4125, loud;
    • the segment left UNSHIFTED at 144000 → the tail is only 96000 frames
      long, never reaches it, plays take 0 throughout → 0.4125, loud;
    • no opening segment synthesised → take 0 until 48000 anyway here, but on
      the head's side window 1 catches the mirror of it.

  WHAT IS STRUCTURAL ONLY, said plainly: the HEAD's truncation. A head that
  keeps segments describing frames it no longer has still PLAYS the same audio,
  because nothing reads a segment past the column's end. So window 1 proves the
  head kept its map and the assert-take-column beside it proves the truncation;
  no render can separate those two. The TAIL's map is the audible half, and
  window 4 is where it bites.

  AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS CASE WAS VACUOUS ON THE TAIL, and it is worth
  saying why. With the map selecting take 0 in window 4, a DROPPED map fell
  back to active take 0 and rendered exactly the same audio, so only the
  structural assertions bit. The map and the active-take fallback have to
  disagree in every window the case asserts, or the render proves nothing.

  ON main THIS FAILS at the first assertion after the split.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="comp_map_survives_split">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <snap-to-grid-disable/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <!-- Take 0: unslipped. Take 1: slipped one second. -->
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_gapsaw.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-take clip="0,0" filePath="../test_gapsaw.wav" startOffset="48000"/>

    <!-- Make take 0 the ACTIVE one, so the map and the fallback disagree.
         select-take clears the map, which is why it comes first. -->
    <select-take clip="0,0" take="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        comp=""/>

    <!-- The comp ALTERNATES, and it has to. See the header: each half needs a
         window where the MAP and the active-take fallback disagree, and only
         windows 1 and 4 separate these two takes at all. So take 1 owns
         window 1 (the head's discriminator) and take 1 owns window 4 (the
         tail's), with take 0 in between. -->
    <set-comp-segment clip="0,0" at="0" take="1"/>
    <set-comp-segment clip="0,0" at="48000" take="0"/>
    <set-comp-segment clip="0,0" at="144000" take="1"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="0:1;48000:0;144000:1" activeTake="0"/>

    <!-- BASELINE. Both windows show the MAP winning over the active take. -->
    <render filename="cmss_pre.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4.0"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="cmss_pre.wav" startFrame="0"
                         frameCount="48000" minRms="0.2100" maxRms="0.2130"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="cmss_pre.wav" startFrame="144000"
                         frameCount="48000" minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.002"/>

    <!-- THE SPLIT, at 2 s. -->
    <split-clip clip="0,0" splitTime="96000"/>

    <!-- Head keeps the segments before the split; tail re-bases to 0, opening
         with whatever sounded AT the split and shifting the rest by -96000. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        comp="0:1;48000:0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,1" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        comp="0:0;48000:1"/>

    <!-- AND THE AUDIO IS UNCHANGED, which is what a split is supposed to be.
         Window 1 is the head's, window 4 is the tail's. -->
    <render filename="cmss_split.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4.0"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="cmss_split.wav" startFrame="0"
                         frameCount="48000" minRms="0.2100" maxRms="0.2130"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="cmss_split.wav" startFrame="144000"
                         frameCount="48000" minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.002"/>

    <!-- UNSPLIT restores the ORIGINAL map, which neither half carries on its
         own: the head's later segments were dropped and the tail's opening one
         was synthesised, so only the splitting action could hand it back. -->
    <undo count="1"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        comp="0:1;48000:0;144000:1"/>
    <render filename="cmss_undone.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4.0"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="cmss_undone.wav" startFrame="0"
                         frameCount="48000" minRms="0.2100" maxRms="0.2130"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="cmss_undone.wav" startFrame="144000"
                         frameCount="48000" minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.002"/>

    <!-- REDO splits again, so the forward is not a one-shot. -->
    <redo count="1"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="0:1;48000:0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,1" comp="0:0;48000:1"/>
  </actions>

</SActionScript>
